HIPAA permits you to share PHI with a business associate only after you obtain "satisfactory assurances," in the form of a BAA, that it will safeguard the information (HHS). According to HHS, a BAA must, among other things:
- describe the permitted and required uses and disclosures of PHI;
- state that the business associate will not use or further disclose PHI except as the agreement permits or the law requires;
- require compliance with applicable Security Rule requirements;
- require reporting of security incidents and breaches of unsecured PHI;
- require that subcontractors agree to the same restrictions through their own BAAs; and
- address return or destruction of PHI at the end of the relationship, where feasible.
HHS publishes sample business associate agreement provisions. Have your counsel review any BAA rather than relying on the agency's template alone.
**Practical tip:** sign the BAA before you create a single login. No PHI should move during interviews or trials without one.

The Security Rule requires security awareness training for all workforce members and appropriate sanctions for people who violate policies (HHS). Ask the agency for:
- HIPAA privacy and security training at onboarding and at least annually;
- phishing and social-engineering awareness;
- client-specific training on your policies, payer portals and escalation paths;
- signed confidentiality agreements; and
- training records you can review.

For each level of care you offer, confirm and record:
- active coverage, plan type and policy dates;
- **in-network and out-of-network benefits** (many treatment centers work out of network, so OON benefits matter);
- deductible, coinsurance, copays and out-of-pocket maximum, plus amounts met to date;
- **authorization and pre-certification requirements**, including who to call and what clinical information is needed;
- day or visit limits and step-down rules;
- whether behavioral health is **carved out** to a separate administrator with a different phone number, portal and payer ID;
- facility vs professional benefits, and any exclusions (for example, for certain residential settings); and
- the payer representative's name, the call reference number, and date and time.
Levels of care commonly verified: detox (withdrawal management), residential, partial hospitalization (PHP), intensive outpatient (IOP) and outpatient. Ask about each one separately; benefits often differ.

VOB work is phone- and portal-based, which makes it well suited to dedicated remote staff, including evening and weekend coverage if you schedule it. What to look for:
- experience with behavioral health payers, carve-outs and out-of-network benefits;
- a written VOB checklist by payer and level of care;
- fast, documented turnaround that admissions can rely on;
- a clean handoff to utilization review and billing; and
- training on HIPAA and Part 2, with a BAA in place before any access (HHS).

The cycle starts before admission. VOB staff verify:
- active coverage and plan type;
- in- and out-of-network benefits by level of care;
- deductibles, coinsurance and out-of-pocket maximums;
- authorization and pre-certification requirements;
- day limits and step-down rules; and
- whether behavioral health is carved out.
Admissions staff need answers fast, often while a prospective patient is on the phone. Speed and accuracy both matter. See eligibility verification and prior authorization.

Medical coders review clinical documentation and assign diagnosis and procedure codes: ICD-10-CM for diagnoses, and CPT and HCPCS for procedures, services and supplies. Typical tasks include:
- coding encounters, procedures or facility claims from provider documentation;
- applying modifiers and checking payer-specific coding rules;
- sending queries to providers when documentation is unclear or incomplete;
- working coding-related denials with the billing team; and
- keeping up with annual and quarterly code-set changes.
Code sets change on a schedule. CMS notes that ICD-10 code updates take effect on October 1 each year (CMS), and CPT is maintained by the AMA's CPT Editorial Panel (AMA). Whoever codes for you needs a routine for updates.

Coders see full clinical documentation, so they're often the most PHI-exposed people on a billing team. Before access:
- a signed business associate agreement with the agency or contractor (HHS);
- unique logins with MFA, and secure remote access through a virtual desktop or VPN;
- no local downloads, printing or USB storage;
- access limited to the charts and functions the role needs; and
- documented HIPAA training.
See our HIPAA guide for outsourced billing staff for the full list of questions to ask. If you're considering offshore coders, read our guide to offshore medical billers for the extra checks.

A virtual assistant who sees schedules, insurance information or billing records handles PHI. Before day one:
- sign a BAA with the staffing provider or contractor (HHS);
- create unique logins with MFA, and use secure remote access through a virtual desktop or VPN;
- grant only the access the tasks require (minimum necessary);
- block local downloads, printing and personal devices; and
- train on HIPAA and your policies, and keep records.
There's no official "HIPAA certified" virtual assistant. See our HIPAA guide for the safeguards to ask about.

A virtual receptionist hears and records patient information on every call. Before day one:
- sign a BAA with the staffing provider (HHS);
- use unique logins with MFA and secure remote access;
- follow minimum necessary access, so the role sees scheduling and demographics but not full charts unless required;
- train on HIPAA and your identity-verification steps for callers; and
- block local downloads and personal devices.
Alliance Global Solutions signs a business associate agreement (BAA) with each client before any of its staff access protected health information. No receptionist or company is "HIPAA certified," because no official certification exists. Ask about specific safeguards using our HIPAA guide.

The same vendor survey asked billers about top denial reasons (Tebra):
- coordination of benefits issues (45% of respondents);
- non-coverage (39%);
- prior authorization and referral issues (31%);
- timely filing errors (26%);
- inaccurate patient demographic information (25%).
In KFF's federal data, insurers cited administrative reasons for 21% of in-network denials and lack of prior authorization or referral for 9%, while the largest category was "Other" at 34% (KFF).
Most of these trace back to the **front end**: eligibility checks, demographics capture and authorizations. That's why denial management and eligibility and prior authorization work belong together.

Payment posting is where you discover underpayments, partial denials and secondary claims that need to go out. Post ERAs daily, reconcile to bank deposits, and flag:
- payments below contracted rates;
- zero-pay lines (these are denials);
- claims needing secondary or tertiary billing; and
- patient responsibility that should move to patient statements.

A billing staffing specialist talks with you, usually for 20 to 30 minutes, about:
- which queues are falling behind (eligibility, authorizations, claims, denials, A/R, posting);
- your systems, payers and specialty-specific rules;
- whether your systems can support secure remote access;
- the hours and coverage you need; and
- whether a remote team is a sensible fit at all.
If it isn't, for example because your processes are undocumented or your payer contracts restrict offshore access, we'll tell you.
